Tamm Bridge house school in Horsham West  Sussex rated outstanding by Ofsted this  secondary like many others is struggling  financially with spending per pupil down  8 percent in real terms since 2010 and  council services being cut by half in  the same period budgets are under  pressure her teacher Jules white has  been working with other school leaders  to lobby for more funding through the  campaign Group worth less it's clear  he's frustrated with the political  response I'm in contact the thousands  have had teachers and they're talking to  me all the time about pressures they  come in all different shapes and sizes  sometimes it's the basic sometimes it's  equipment sometimes it's building often  though it's about staffing and capacity  levels all school budgets 90% are  wrapped up within staffing if you  haven't got the money then you don't  have the staffing levels class sizes go  up you can't provide the type of  one-to-one support particularly for  youngsters with special needs or those  from disadvantaged backgrounds  we're squeezed and as one head teacher  said to me I just don't sleep at night  it's a constant source of worry and what  kind of difficult decisions have you had  to make as well  the biggest concern I've had over the  last year is trying to provide the type  of support that young people with  special educational needs require I have  21 children who have a educational  health and care plans this statement is  providing them with additional support  but I actually only have 18 full-time  equivalent ta so I don't even give their  ta is their teacher teaching assistant  and I don't even have the one-to-one  support for those youngsters but I'm a  school of 1600 I've got one or two  hundred other children who have  particular learning needs what do I do  about them I can't put teaching  assistants within a classroom I can't  meet the needs that those youngsters and  families they're just legitimate and I  feel as though I'm letting people down  and that's not fair all the time my  staff are being asked to do more and  more with much less I mean the  government say that one is going up so  yeah well I mean it's the same old same  old isn't it yes money is going up there  also 3/4 of a million more children in  our school since 2010 that's why the  money is going up in real terms the  money is going down we need really  significant investment do you think it  will perhaps decide the next election I  think it will have a profound effect and  I think that that is why it's talked  about so much but we need all of the  parties to actually recognize the  important education but come up with  coherent plans as well most head teacher  will tell you we want education to be  free of political interference we're  really good professionals fund us  properly resources properly give us some  teachers and we will do the rest  these four boys benefit from a  specialist literacy class every week  they're studying Charlie and the  Chocolate Factory the school would like  to provide more lessons like this but  they don't have the staff 14 percent of  pupils at Tam Bridge house are on the  special needs register but the number of  teaching assistants has gone down  because of budget pressures we could be  talking about children that have got  autism perhaps ADHD dyslexic students  but of course we've also got a lot of  students who might have special  educational needs but also it's their  well-being needs and and all the  pressures increasing they are because we  have to do more of what we in the past  would have been able to rely on outside  agencies for watching what kind of a  speech and language therapist and we do  have a great therapist who comes in once  twice a year but it's only to work with  very specific students and is that  because the council budgets are going  down as well yes the county can't  provide those resources anymore and we  can't afford to buy them in either so  we're having to do ourselves in  Westminster the Education Select  Committee has been investigating special  needs provision and its chair Robert  Halfin is worried by what he's found  every day in our country there are  thousands of parents struggling  who have children with special  educational needs literally struggling  wading through treacle trying to get the  right provision the right care the right  education for their child with special  educational and needs and it's wrong  this is wrong if the National Health  Service can have a ten year plan if the  National Self Service can have twenty  billion extra of taxpayer funding which  I welcome  why on earth why on earth does an  education have a ten year plan  why on earth can't education get a five  year funding settlement a spokesperson  from the Department for Education said  the Education Secretary has made it  clear that high-needs funding is  priority and he recognizes the  challenges that local authorities and  schools face that's why he is committed  to working with the sector on this as we  approach the spending review in December  we provided an extra 250 million pounds  up to 2020 to help local authorities  manage high needs cost pressures this  takes the total allocated for high needs  to six point three billion pounds this  year compared to five billion in 2013  Tam Bridge House is clearly an  outstanding school but it's operating  under a seriously stretched budget and  they're not alone we've seen emails from  schools in Staffordshire Lancashire  Essex and Somerset and they're all  saying the same thing but they don't  have the money to fully support children  with complex needs in the way they want  it with a conservative leadership  contest looming and brexit still unsold  school funding is at risk of dropping  down the political agenda but if there  is a general election before 2022 its  issues like education that could decide  the outcome
 

